There was something called tex2tty, or dvi2tty, or something
like that.  It worked pretty much as you describe: it created a
grid for the characters in the output, read each character in
the dvi file, calculated its position, and put it into the grid
cell which was closest.  I found, however, that by carefully
choosing a fixed width default font and setting the sizes for
spaces correctly, the results were exactly what I wanted.  (I
had to modify it to handle ISO 8859-1 correctly, but otherwise,
no problems.)  At the time, I maintained my CV (full of table
data) as a LaTeX source, included by one of either two LaTeX
sources, one used to produce Postscript (with dvips), and the
other to produce pure ASCII (or ISO 8859-1, rather), for
including in emails, etc.  The results were extremely
satisfactory.

